  "query_preview": "..The weak, 500 mb cut-off low is currently located \nover west-central AZ and will continue to shift southward through\nthe evening, and exiting AZ by Saturday morning. This is\nsupporting scattered showers and a few embedded thunderstorms,\nmainly across Navajo and Apache counties, where southeast to \nsouthwest 850-500 mb winds have allowed for  transport of \nmoisture in the region. A a couple isolated showers and \nthunderstorms are also starting to develop to the west of \nFlagstaff, near and south of Williams and Ash Fork, where \ninstability is starting to increase due colder air aloft, positive\nvorticity advection and upper-level divergence just south of the \n500 mb low starting to increase the instability in the area. As \nthe 500 mb low continues to shift southward, the development of \nshowers and thunderstorms will also shift southward. Rainfall \namounts are expected to remain light and generally around 0.10\" to\n0.20\", with a few locally higher amounts between 0.25\" to 0.70\" \nin heavier showers and thunderstorms. As the low shifts south, \ncolder air will be drawn down to the surface and some snow may \nbegin to mix in. Snow levels look to be mostly near or above \n8000', including areas like Buffalo Pass in the Chuskas, with \nlittle to no accumulation expected.\n\nFor Saturday and Sunday, as the 500 mb low continues to move \nsouth of AZ through the weekend, the pressure gradient will \nstrengthen, creating breezy conditions, especially across the \nhigher terrain. Winds will be out of the north to northeast around\n5-15 mph in most areas with gusts to 20-35 mph along and \nsouth/southwest of the higher terrain. \n\nMonday through Thursday, a mostly dry and mild weather pattern is\nexpected. A weak short-wave trough is expected to cross through \nsouthern Arizona on Tuesday, but moisture will be very limited and\nis unlikely that more than an isolated shower or two will \ndevelop, mainly near the White Mountains. Looking further into the\nweekend, ensemble models continue to show a deepening west coast \ntrough that becomes a cut-off low as it moves over the United \nStates. Most models continue to favor a souther track, keeping the\nlow sufficiently south to limit moisture returns into the region.\nHowever, a shift northward could bring another round of showers \nto our area.",
